= Resistor =

== Overview ==
A '''resistor''' is an electronic component designed to provide a specified electrical resistance in a circuit.

== Definition ==
Resistance relates voltage and current. In an ohmic component under appropriate conditions, Ohm's law expresses the relationship as V = IR, where V is voltage, I is current, and R is resistance.

== Scope & importance ==
Resistors make currents and voltage drops controllable. They are among the fundamental components used to bias, divide, limit, sense, terminate, and dissipate electrical energy in circuits.

== Core concepts ==
Important characteristics include resistance value, tolerance, power rating, temperature coefficient, noise, and construction type.

== Examples ==
A series resistor can limit current through an LED. Two resistors can form a voltage divider. A precision shunt resistor can be used to infer current from a measured voltage drop.

== Applications / implications ==
Resistors appear in analog and digital electronics, power systems, sensing, signal conditioning, timing networks, feedback networks, and circuit protection.

== Epistemic notes ==
The ideal resistor is a model. Real components have parasitic capacitance and inductance, finite power limits, temperature dependence, and manufacturing tolerance.

